[DEBUG] 2026-10-07 04:07:29,040 views | Parsed html_data: {'title': 'two-sum-problem-unique', 'legend': '<p>You are given an array of integers $$$a_1, a_2, \\dots, a_n$$$ and an integer target $$$t$$$.</p><p>Find two <span class="tex-font-style-bf">different</span> positions $$$i$$$ and $$$j$$$ ($$$i \\ne j$$$) such that $$$a_i + a_j = t$$$.</p><p>It is guaranteed that exactly one such pair of positions exists. The same element cannot be used twice, but two different positions may contain equal values. You may print the two indices in any order.</p>', 'input_format': '<p>The first line contains two integers $$$n$$$ and $$$t$$$ ($$$2 \\le n \\le 10^4$$$, $$$-10^9 \\le t \\le 10^9$$$) — the number of elements in the array and the target sum.</p><p>The second line contains $$$n$$$ integers $$$a_1, a_2, \\dots, a_n$$$ ($$$-10^9 \\le a_i \\le 10^9$$$) — the elements of the array.</p><p>It is guaranteed that there is exactly one pair of distinct indices $$$(i, j)$$$ such that $$$a_i + a_j = t$$$.</p>', 'output_format': '<p>Print two distinct integers — the $$$1$$$-based indices $$$i$$$ and $$$j$$$ such that $$$a_i + a_j = t$$$. You may print them in any order.</p>', 'notes': '<p>In the first example, $$$a_1 + a_2 = 2 + 7 = 9$$$, so the answer is <span class="tex-font-style-tt">1 2</span>.</p><p>In the second example, $$$a_2 + a_3 = 2 + 4 = 6$$$. Note that $$$a_1 = 3$$$ cannot be paired with itself, so <span class="tex-font-style-tt">1 1</span> is not a valid answer.</p><p>In the third example, both elements are equal to $$$3$$$, and since they are at different positions, <span class="tex-font-style-tt">1 2</span> is a valid answer.</p>'}
